# Locks, alarms, covers and valves

These controls can change access or move equipment. Present the named object, its reported state and the specific intended effect together. A card colour, modal confirmation or hidden button never establishes permission or physical safety.

**HA-CTRL-006 — reference design specification.** This specifies the local design coverage requested by HA-GAP-006 for an existing Home Assistant capability. The examples do not install a native adapter, connect to a backend or operate equipment.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-006-capabilities">Task and capability contract</h2>

| Equipment | Capability and action contract |
| --- | --- |
| Lock | Distinguish Lock, Unlock and supported Open/latch release. Preserve protected calls, code requirements and native confirmation. |
| Alarm panel | Offer only supported arming modes and the native code flow. Arming, pending and triggered states are not a generic on/off switch. |
| Cover | Preserve open/close, optional position/tilt and supported Stop. A partially open cover is not necessarily fully open. |
| Valve | Preserve device class, optional position and supported Stop. Position is not measured flow or proof of isolation. |
| Permission or code absent | Explain the real restriction through the native flow; do not store a substitute code in card configuration or documentation. |

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-006-states">States, pending work and failure</h2>

Keep locked, unlocked, locking, unlocking, open, opening, jammed, unknown and unavailable distinct when the integration supplies them. Do not equate an unlocked lock with an open door. A separate contact sensor can provide door position, but it remains a separate observation.

During cover or valve movement, keep Stop reachable if supported. Dismissing a review or leaving the page does not stop movement. A rejected request leaves the latest reported state visible; a waiting limit yields an unconfirmed result, not automatic success or reversal. Do not retry a jammed lock, close a valve or disarm an alarm automatically to repair the UI.

Alarm delays follow the panel's reported state and timing. Do not invent a countdown from a generic delay assumption. A camera image, presence report or closed-valve icon must not become a “Home secure” assertion.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-006-content">Content and interpretation</h2>

Name the object in the action: “Unlock front door”, “Stop study blind”. Explain latch release separately from unlocking where relevant. Use “Reported closed” if the evidence is only a device report. Keep code prompts private and do not echo codes into notifications, logs, examples or screenshots. Preserve native translations and source fault wording.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-006-accessibility">Accessibility and interaction</h2>

Provide a keyboard route to every action and keep Stop visible during movement. A review dialog needs an accessible name, logical focus order, Escape/dismiss behaviour and return focus. The local dialog demonstrates only review presentation. Do not stack a whole-card tap action over dedicated lock buttons. Use persistent text for faults; reserve assertive announcements for a defined urgent condition.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-006-integration">Native integration boundary</h2>

Retain native More info and its protected lock/alarm implementation. No new access-control layer is supplied here. Backend permission rejection must remain effective even if presentation is bypassed. Home Assistant does not replace physical safety checks, a certified alarm or equipment-specific procedures. The source review informs this recipe; connected equipment and its installed integration require separate acceptance evidence.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-006-examples">Reference examples</h2>

These complete, isolated HTML specimens use synthetic data. Buttons and forms only demonstrate local feedback or draft validation. Static state examples are labelled as such; no action reaches a household.

<%= example('ha-access-lock') %>

<%= example('ha-access-moving') %>

<%= example('ha-access-fault') %>

<%= example('ha-access-alarm-valve') %>

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-006-acceptance">Acceptance and evidence</h2>

1. Verify each supported lock transition and jammed/unknown/unavailable state. Unlock must not become Open unless the source reports it.
2. Use an unauthorised session and an invalid code in a controlled test environment; the backend must reject the action independently of card visibility. Never use real secrets in evidence.
3. Test cover/valve Stop during motion, a missing Stop feature, partial position and delayed confirmation. Dismissal must not claim cancellation.
4. Check keyboard review/return focus, shared-screen privacy and long object names. Do not perform a physical security action merely to test a colour or layout.

Record the integration/frontend version, browser, fixture or device, input state, expected result, observed result and evidence location. Source review and documentation checks are not live-device verification. Record unresolved native limitations explicitly; do not turn this checklist into an accessibility guarantee.

